./docs/adr/0021-test-invitations-are-a-separate-token-kind-and-are-never-saved.md:6:- A test token is derived from `INVITATION_TOKEN_KEY` like an Invitation token, but under its own label: `test-invitation:v1:<pulseId>:<personId>:<testId>`, where `testId` is random per send. A test token can therefore never equal an Invitation token, and the key still never leaves the Worker environment.
./docs/adr/0021-test-invitations-are-a-separate-token-kind-and-are-never-saved.md:7:- Only the token's hash is stored, in its own D1 table `test_invitation_index` (hash, Organization, Pulse, expiry). It is never written to `invitation_index`, and the Organization has no Invitation row for it. A test token expires 7 days after it is sent. Expired rows in both test tables are deleted whenever a new test goes out. Removing a Person deletes their `test_send` rows; their unexpired index rows hold no Person and simply lapse.
./docs/adr/0021-test-invitations-are-a-separate-token-kind-and-are-never-saved.md:8:- The Organization stores only the send in `test_send` (send id, Person, Pulse, token hash, time) for the rate limit and for the Queue consumer to index the token once the email is sent, as it does for Invitations. Rows older than 7 days are deleted.
./docs/adr/0021-test-invitations-are-a-separate-token-kind-and-are-never-saved.md:29:- `test_send` stores a Person and a time, but nothing about answers.

